1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Almost everything you do with a model is measured in tokens,

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,000
from what fits in a single request,

3
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:07,000
to what shows up on your bill.

4
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:09,000
We'll talk through what a token actually is,

5
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,000
how the context window budgets those tokens,

6
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,000
and how big that window is on today's models.

7
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:19,000
Let's jump in. Let's lock

8
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:20,000
down three definitions we'll lean

9
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,000
on for the rest of this material.

10
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,000
A token is the atomic unit of text the model reads and generates.

11
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:27,000
It's a sub-word chunk,

12
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:29,000
usually just a few characters long,

13
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:31,000
not a whole word.

14
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:33,000
The context window is the maximum number of

15
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:36,000
tokens the model can attend to in one request.

16
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Think of it as the total working space for a single call,

17
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:42,000
and the token budget is the idea that everything you

18
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:44,000
send plus everything the model

19
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,000
generates draws from that one shared pool.

20
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Keep those three straight and the rest

21
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,000
of this lesson clicks into place.

22
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Because tokens, the window,

23
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:54,000
and the budget are really one system.

24
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Here's the first thing that trips people up.

25
00:00:57,000 --> 00:00:59,000
A token isn't a word.

26
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:02,000
On average, one token runs about four characters,

27
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:06,000
which works out to a bit over half a word per token.

28
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,000
A thousand words is very loosely around 1,800 tokens.

29
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:12,000
But that ratio is only a rule of thumb.

30
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,000
It shifts with the language you're working in,

31
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:17,000
and it shifts a lot with code,

32
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:19,000
punctuation, and unusual formatting.

33
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:21,000
A block of dense JSON

34
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:24,000
tokenizes very differently from plain prose.

35
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,000
The takeaway is that a word count is a loose proxy at best.

36
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:29,000
When you actually need to know

37
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:31,000
how many tokens a prompt will cost,

38
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,000
you don't want to be guessing from word counts.

39
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:37,000
So how do you count tokens the right way?

40
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:38,000
You've got a spectrum here.

41
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:40,000
At the loose end is the rough estimate,

42
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:45,000
about four characters or a bit over half a word per token.

43
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,000
That's fine when you just want a ballpark.

44
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,000
At the precise end is the count token's endpoint,

45
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:53,000
which returns the exact number

46
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:55,000
for the specific model you pass it.

47
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:56,000
That's the authoritative source,

48
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:58,000
and it's what I'd reach for

49
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,000
before sizing a real prompt or estimating cost.

50
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:04,000
What you want to avoid is guessing

51
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,000
with a fixed characters per token rule

52
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:09,000
or a tokenizer built for some other model.

53
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,000
Those drift from Claude's real counts, sometimes badly,

54
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,000
and the drift tends to be worst on code.

55
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:18,000
There's a wrinkle that catches people off guard

56
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,000
when they upgrade.

57
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:22,000
The tokenizer isn't fixed across models.

58
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:27,000
Opus 4.7 and later, plus Fable 5 and Sonnet 5,

59
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:30,000
split text more finely than earlier models did.

60
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:32,000
For the exact same input,

61
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:36,000
the newer tokenizer can produce roughly 30% more tokens.

62
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:37,000
That's a big deal,

63
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:40,000
because it means your token counts are model-specific.

64
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:42,000
A prompt you measured on an older model

65
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:44,000
can suddenly cost noticeably more

66
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:46,000
once you move to a current one,

67
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,000
even though not a single character changed.

68
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,000
So whenever you switch models, rebaseline.

69
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Run the count tokens endpoint again for the new model

70
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:58,000
and adjust your context budgets and cost estimates to match.

71
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,000
Don't assume last month's numbers still hold.

72
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:04,000
Now, how big is this window?

73
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
It depends entirely on the model.

74
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:09,000
Fable 5, Opus 4.8, and Sonnet 5

75
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:12,000
each attend to up to 1 million tokens.

76
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:15,000
And that window is one shared pool.

77
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,000
So the model's own output and thinking

78
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:21,000
draw from the same 1 million budget as your input.

79
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:24,000
That's enormous, a bit over half a million English words,

80
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:29,000
around 555,000 in a single request.

81
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:33,000
Haiku 4.5 is smaller at 200,000 tokens

82
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:36,000
or roughly 150,000 words.

83
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,000
That's still a lot, but it's a fifth of the big models.

84
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:43,000
So the same oversized prompt might sail through on Opus

85
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:45,000
and Overflow on Haiku.

86
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:48,000
So name the model before you reason about capacity,

87
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:51,000
because choosing the smaller, cheaper model

88
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,000
means a tighter budget you have to plan around.

89
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:57,000
It helps to picture what actually fills that window,

90
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:00,000
because it's a lot more than your latest question.

91
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:02,000
The system prompt sits in there.

92
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Every tool definition you've registered takes up space.

93
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:08,000
The entire conversation history counts,

94
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:12,000
and so does every tool result the model has seen.

95
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
And then the tokens the model is generating right now

96
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:17,000
come out of the same budget too.

97
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,000
All of it shares one pool.

98
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,000
That's why a long agent run can slowly creep

99
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:26,000
toward the limit, even if each individual message is short.

100
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:28,000
The history keeps growing.

101
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:29,000
So when you think about context,

102
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,000
don't just count the user's prompt.

103
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:34,000
Count everything the model is carrying

104
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,000
on this particular call.

105
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:39,000
Tokens get counted in a few buckets,

106
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:42,000
and knowing them helps you read your usage and your bill.

107
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Input tokens are everything you send in,

108
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,000
the system prompt, the history, your tool definitions,

109
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,000
and the current user turn.

110
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Output tokens are everything the model generates,

111
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,000
and that's more than the visible reply.

112
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:59,000
They bill at a higher rate than input.

113
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:02,000
Thinking tokens on models that support reasoning

114
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:07,000
are billed as output tokens and included in the output total.

115
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:10,000
The usage object reports them as a read-only breakdown

116
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:13,000
inside that total, so you can see how much of your output

117
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:16,000
went to reasoning rather than the final answer.

118
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:18,000
Everything still draws down the same window,

119
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:21,000
and it all shows up when you model your spend.

120
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:24,000
What happens when you run out of room?

121
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:26,000
This is the part people get wrong.

122
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:28,000
Overflowing the window isn't something

123
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:30,000
the model quietly fixes for you.

124
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:33,000
There's no automatic summarization at the boundary.

125
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:35,000
If you exceed the limit, you get an error,

126
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:38,000
and it's on you to stay under budget.

127
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,000
You do that by pruning old turns

128
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:43,000
or compacting history into a shorter form.

129
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,000
One reassuring note on cost, though.

130
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:48,000
A full one million token request bills

131
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:52,000
at the standard per token rate on the current large models.

132
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:54,000
There's no long context surcharge

133
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:56,000
for using the whole window,

134
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:59,000
but standard rate on a million tokens still adds up,

135
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:03,000
so a big window is a budget to manage, not free space.

136
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:08,000
Tokens are the unit for what fits and what you pay.

137
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:11,000
A token is a sub-word chunk, not a word.

138
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:15,000
The window spans prompt, history, tools, and generation.

139
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:18,000
Keep in mind, Fable, Opus, and Sonnet hold a million tokens,

140
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:23,000
while Haiku holds 200,000, so count per model.

141
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,000
That covers tokens and the context window budget.

142
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:27,000
I'll see you in the next lesson.

